[{"id":"30621","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which of the following years were the Mahatma Gandhi Series notes launched?</span></p>","option_1":"1976","option_2":"1996","option_3":"1992","option_4":"1984","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - 1996.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Reserve Bank of India introduced the Mahatma Gandhi Series of currency notes in 1996, replacing the earlier Ashoka Pillar watermark series. This series was later followed by the Mahatma Gandhi New Series launched in 2016, after demonetisation. Currency-related facts such as this are frequently asked in the Banking/Economy portion of SSC General Awareness.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30622","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which among the following is the early king of Magadha?</span></p>","option_1":"Ajatashatru","option_2":"Dhana Nanda","option_3":"Samudra Gupta","option_4":"Mahapadma Nanda","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Ajatashatru.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Ajatashatru of the Haryanka dynasty (son of Bimbisara) was one of the early and powerful kings of Magadha, ruling around the 5th century BCE. He expanded Magadha's territory by defeating the Licchavis of Vaishali and the kingdom of Kosala. Dhana Nanda and Mahapadma Nanda belong to the later Nanda dynasty, while Samudragupta belongs to the Gupta dynasty (4th century CE) — both considerably later than the early Magadhan kings.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30623","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In what language were the Vedic hymns composed?</span></p>","option_1":"Pali","option_2":"Prakrit","option_3":"Tamil","option_4":"Sanskrit","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Sanskrit.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The four Vedas — Rigveda, Samaveda, Yajurveda, and Atharvaveda — were composed in Vedic Sanskrit, an early form of Sanskrit, roughly between 1500 and 500 BCE. Pali and Prakrit developed later and are chiefly associated with Buddhist and Jain literature respectively, while Tamil belongs to the Dravidian language family and is unrelated to Vedic composition.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30624","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who among the following can impeach the President of India?</span></p>","option_1":"Parliament","option_2":"Prime Minister of India","option_3":"Supreme Court of India","option_4":"Election Commission of India","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Parliament.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per Article 61 of the Indian Constitution, the President of India can be impeached only by Parliament (both the Lok Sabha and the Rajya Sabha) on the ground of \"violation of the Constitution.\" The impeachment resolution must be passed by a two-thirds majority of the total membership of each House after due procedure. Neither the Prime Minister, the Supreme Court, nor the Election Commission has the power to impeach the President.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30625","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The nervous tissue is made up of an organised network of _____.</span></p>","option_1":"Neurons","option_2":"Capillary","option_3":"Lymph vessels","option_4":"Epithelial cells","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Neurons.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Nervous tissue is composed of an organised network of neurons (nerve cells) — specialised cells that generate and transmit electrical/chemical signals — along with supporting glial cells. Capillaries and lymph vessels belong to the circulatory and lymphatic systems respectively, and epithelial cells form the lining/covering tissue of organs, so none of these constitute nervous tissue.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30626","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which province did Ahmad Shah Abdali seize from the Mughals?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Punjab</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Awadh</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Only I.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Ahmad Shah Abdali (Durrani), the Afghan invader, seized Punjab from the Mughal Empire following his repeated invasions of India in the mid-18th century (notably after 1752), bringing Punjab under Afghan control until the rise of Sikh power there. Awadh remained under the control of its own Nawabs (a Mughal-affiliated regional dynasty) and was never seized by Abdali. Hence, only statement I is correct.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30627","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What is the formula to calculate the weight of an object?</span></p>","option_1":"W= m × g","option_2":"W= m / g","option_3":"W= m / (g× g)","option_4":"W= m × g × g","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - W= m × g.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Weight (W) is the force exerted on a body due to gravity and is given by the formula W = m × g, where m is the mass of the object and g is the acceleration due to gravity (approximately 9.8 m/s² on the Earth's surface). This distinguishes weight (a force, measured in newtons) from mass (measured in kilograms), a frequently tested distinction in SSC science sections.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30628","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Urali language is spoken mainly in _____.</span></p>","option_1":"Northern India","option_2":"Western India","option_3":"Eastern India","option_4":"Southern India","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Southern India.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Urali is a Dravidian language spoken by the Urali tribal community, found mainly in the states of Kerala and Tamil Nadu — both located in Southern India. Questions on lesser-known tribal/regional languages and their geographic distribution are commonly asked under SSC's \"Art &amp; Culture/Society\" static GK segment.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30629","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which of the following places was the first Singh Sabha formed?</span></p>","option_1":"Delhi","option_2":"Karachi","option_3":"Amritsar","option_4":"Hyderabad","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Amritsar.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Singh Sabha Movement, a Sikh revivalist and reform movement, began with the establishment of the first Singh Sabha at Amritsar in 1873. It aimed to counter the growing influence of Christian missionaries and the Arya Samaj, and to revive Sikh religious, cultural, and educational values.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30630","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In 'Burrakatha', the lead singer plays which instrument?</span></p>","option_1":"Flute","option_2":"Guitar","option_3":"Veena","option_4":"Tambura","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Tambura.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Burrakatha is a traditional oral storytelling and performing art form from Andhra Pradesh and Telangana, in which the lead narrator plays the tambura (a stringed drone instrument) while narrating stories — often on historical, social, or mythological themes — accompanied by two supporting performers who provide comic relief and rhythm.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30631","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What is the population range for a Class-I city as per the Census of India?</span></p>","option_1":"1 lakh and above","option_2":"10,000 - 19,999","option_3":"Below 5,000","option_4":"50,000 - 99,999","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - 1 lakh and above.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>As per the Census of India's urban classification, towns/cities are grouped into six classes based on population. Class-I towns/cities are those with a population of 1,00,000 (1 lakh) and above. This classification (Class I to Class VI) is a frequently tested topic under SSC's Indian Geography/Demography section.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30632","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In which city is Reliance Industries collaborating with Google to build a dedicated cloud facility powered by clean energy?</span></p>","option_1":"Patna","option_2":"Amritsar","option_3":"Jamnagar","option_4":"Cuttack","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Jamnagar.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Reliance Industries has partnered with Google to set up a dedicated, clean-energy-powered cloud/AI data-centre facility at Jamnagar, Gujarat, leveraging Reliance's renewable energy infrastructure already established in the region. This is a current-affairs-based question drawn from recent corporate/technology news.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30633","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What is used to determine the density of liquids?</span></p>","option_1":"Lactometer","option_2":"Hydrometer","option_3":"Ammeter","option_4":"Barometer","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Hydrometer.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A hydrometer is an instrument used to measure the density (specific gravity) of liquids, working on the principle of buoyancy (Archimedes' Principle). A lactometer specifically checks the purity/density of milk, an ammeter measures electric current, and a barometer measures atmospheric pressure — none of which measure general liquid density.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30634","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In India, the tertiary coal occurs in which of the following state?</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>I. Meghalaya</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>II. Assam</span></p>","option_1":"Only I","option_2":"Only II","option_3":"Both I and II","option_4":"Neither I nor II","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Both I and II.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Tertiary coal (younger than the more extensive Gondwana/Permian coal deposits) in India is found mainly in the North-Eastern states — Meghalaya, Assam, Nagaland, and Arunachal Pradesh. Hence, both Meghalaya and Assam have tertiary coal deposits, making \"Both I and II\" the correct answer.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30635","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who is the author of the book 'Second Thoughts'?</span></p>","option_1":"Sudha Murty","option_2":"Shobha De","option_3":"Chetan Bhagat","option_4":"Faraaz Kazi","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - Shobha De.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'Second Thoughts' is a novel written by Indian author Shobha De, known for contemporary fiction set against the backdrop of urban Indian society, relationships, and glamour. Author-book pairings such as this are regularly tested under SSC's \"Books &amp; Authors\" static GK segment.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30636","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>What commitment did India make as part of its globalisation efforts during LPG reforms?</span></p>","option_1":"To increase tariffs on import of commodities.","option_2":"To abolish or reduce tariffs on the import of commodities.","option_3":"To impose quantitative restrictions on imports.","option_4":"To increase non-tariff barriers on imports.","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - To abolish or reduce tariffs on the import of commodities..</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Under the Liberalisation, Privatisation, and Globalisation (LPG) reforms initiated in 1991, India committed to reducing trade barriers — including abolishing or significantly lowering tariffs on imports — to integrate with the global economy, attract foreign investment, and honour its commitments under GATT/WTO. This is the opposite of raising tariffs, imposing quantitative restrictions, or adding non-tariff barriers, which are protectionist measures.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30637","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who is the author of the book 'The Vault of Vishnu'?</span></p>","option_1":"Ashwin Sanghi","option_2":"Akash Banerjee","option_3":"Rohit Lal","option_4":"Tanya Selvaratnam","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Ashwin Sanghi.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>'The Vault of Vishnu' is a mythological-thriller novel by Ashwin Sanghi, an author well known for blending Indian mythology and history with contemporary thriller narratives (as in \"The Rozabal Line,\" \"The Krishna Key,\" and \"Chanakya's Chant\").</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30638","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Shin guard is the term primarily related to which of the following sports?</span></p>","option_1":"Badminton","option_2":"Long jump","option_3":"Football","option_4":"Handball","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"3","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option C - Football.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>A shin guard is protective equipment worn over the front of the leg (shin) to protect players from injuries caused by kicks and collisions. It is an essential piece of equipment primarily used in football (soccer), and is a commonly tested \"sports equipment/terminology\" question in SSC exams.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30639","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which of the following forts is located in Rajasthan?</span></p>","option_1":"Gwalior Fort","option_2":"Sinhagad Fort","option_3":"Daulatabad Fort","option_4":"Bharatpur Fort","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Bharatpur Fort.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Bharatpur Fort (also known as Lohagarh Fort) is located in Bharatpur, Rajasthan, and is historically notable for successfully repelling British attacks in the early 19th century. Gwalior Fort is in Madhya Pradesh, while Sinhagad Fort and Daulatabad Fort are both located in Maharashtra.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30640","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Prime Minister of which of the following country was the chief guest of 10th edition of the Raisina Dialogue?</span></p>","option_1":"Brazil","option_2":"New Zealand","option_3":"Italy","option_4":"France","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"2","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option B - New Zealand.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The Prime Minister of New Zealand was the chief guest at the 10th edition of the Raisina Dialogue — India's flagship annual conference on geopolitics and geo-economics, hosted in New Delhi. This reflects India's deepening strategic engagement with Indo-Pacific nations and is drawn from recent current affairs.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30641","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The opening ceremony of Khelo India Winter Games 2024 (KIWG) was held in _____.</span></p>","option_1":"Leh","option_2":"Jammu","option_3":"Kargil","option_4":"Darjeeling","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Leh.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The opening ceremony of the Khelo India Winter Games (KIWG) 2024 was held in Leh, Union Territory of Ladakh — a region well suited for winter sports such as ice hockey, skiing, and skating owing to its high-altitude, snow-covered terrain. This event forms part of the Khelo India initiative to promote sports at the grassroots level.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30642","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Which ancient city, believed to have been built by the Pandavas, is considered an early part of present-day Delhi?</span></p>","option_1":"Ayodhya","option_2":"Hastinapur","option_3":"Mathura","option_4":"Indraprastha","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"4","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option D - Indraprastha.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>According to legend and the epic Mahabharata, Indraprastha was the capital city built by the Pandavas and is traditionally regarded as an early settlement forming part of present-day Delhi. Ayodhya and Mathura are associated with other epics/deities, and Hastinapur was the capital of the Kuru dynasty, located in present-day Uttar Pradesh.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30643","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>In February 1999, the International Olympic Committee (IOC) convened the First World Conference on Doping in Sport in _____.</span></p>","option_1":"Switzerland","option_2":"France","option_3":"Germany","option_4":"Poland","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Switzerland.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>The IOC convened the First World Conference on Doping in Sport in Lausanne, Switzerland, in February 1999. This conference was a landmark event that led directly to the establishment of the World Anti-Doping Agency (WADA) later that same year, to coordinate the global fight against doping in sport.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30644","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Coke is the almost pure form of _____.</span></p>","option_1":"carbon","option_2":"phosphorus","option_3":"sulphur","option_4":"nitrogen","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - carbon.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Coke is obtained by the destructive distillation of coal — heating coal in the absence of air to drive off volatile impurities — and is an almost pure form of carbon. It is widely used as a fuel and as a reducing agent in metallurgical processes such as the extraction of iron in blast furnaces.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""},{"id":"30645","test_series_id":"2","test_id":"814","question_type":"1","question_ui_type":"1","question":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Who is the first woman to be awarded the Nobel Prize twice?</span></p>","option_1":"Marie Curie","option_2":"Golda Meir","option_3":"Rosa Parks","option_4":"Mother Teresa","option_5":"","option_6":"","option_7":"","option_8":"","option_9":"","option_10":"","option_image_1":"","option_image_2":"","option_image_3":"","option_image_4":"","option_image_5":"","option_image_6":"","option_image_7":"","option_image_8":"","option_image_9":"","option_image_10":"","image_link_1":"","image_link_2":"","image_link_3":"","answer":"1","solution_heading":"Full Solution","solution_text":" <p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Correct Answer: Option A - Marie Curie.</span></p><p style=\"margin-top:0pt;margin-bottom:0pt;font-size:12pt\"><span>Marie Curie was the first woman — and indeed the first person ever — to be awarded the Nobel Prize twice: in Physics (1903, shared, for research on radioactivity) and in Chemistry (1911, for the discovery of the elements radium and polonium). She remains the only person to win Nobel Prizes in two different scientific fields.</span></p>","solution_image_1":"","solution_image_2":"","solution_video":"","difficulty_level":"0","topic":"","exam":"","section_id":"4","subject":"","concept":"","report_count":"0","set_no":"0","positive_marks":"3.00","negative_marks":"1.00","sortingparam":"0.00","question_heading":"","directive":"","deleted":"0","image_check":"0","subject_title":"","concept_title":"","topic_title":""}]